# LnFinder — checksums of the archived data set
#
# sha256sum format: one line per file, "<digest>  <path>", the path relative to the DATA
# ROOT ($LNFINDER_DATA, or dat/ in the clone). Lines beginning with # are comments, so
# this file can be checked either with `scripts/verify_data.jl` or, from inside the data
# root, with `sha256sum -c` (`shasum -a 256 -c` on macOS).
#
# WHAT IS HERE. The digests of the archived files, recorded when those files were
# assembled for deposit, so that a data root can be checked against this repository and
# not only against itself. The archive carries its own MANIFEST.sha256, and that copy is
# the complete one: scripts/verify_data.jl checks a data root against it when it is there,
# and then CROSS-CHECKS this file for the paths it covers. A disagreement between the two
# is a failure in its own right — which of them records the bytes on disk cannot be decided
# from inside the archive, and an archive that agrees only with itself has established
# nothing. That second, independent location is the whole reason this file is shipped.
#
# The two section comments below are EXACT markers, not prose to be read for its sense. A
# manifest assembled with the archive must copy them verbatim; an unrecognised "# ---"
# comment makes verify_data.jl refuse to read the file rather than guess, because guessing
# is how a required file silently becomes an optional one.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. Two of the habitat route's inputs are not in the archive
# at all, so neither this file nor the archive's own manifest records a digest of them:
# the relation cache's stored response bodies, one per taxon and named for that taxon,
# and the ontology path cache beside them. Both are third-party content rather than this
# project's output — the bodies are a text-mining service's own responses, the path cache
# a verbatim extract of an ontology's own structure — and neither is redistributed here.
# What is recorded in their place is the digest of the cache's own manifest.csv, which
# says which taxa were fetched, by which route and when, and carries none of the responses.
# The deposit section below carries a digest for every archived file except one,
# structures/structure_accessions.txt, and the paragraph below gives the reason.
#
# criteria/category_selection.csv is listed below. Its digest is that of the file the
# archive carries, taken from the copy the archive is assembled from, so this record and
# the archive's own manifest name the same bytes for it and a reader can check it against
# two independent records like every other deposited file.
#
# The anchor targets step 4 checks its grid against, and the two files they were transcribed
# from, are not here because they are not in the archive: they are tracked in this
# repository under dat/criteria/, and step 4 checks the two sources against the digests the
# targets file declares.
#
# The structure accession list is in the archive, and its digest is recorded in the
# archive's own manifest rather than here: it is one identifier per member of the census,
# so a list of them is the census's membership, and an integrity record that ships with
# the code is not the place to publish it. The twelve experimental structures under
# optional DO have digests, and that is a considered distinction rather than an oversight:
# twelve published crystal entries, which the accompanying publication names in any case,
# are not a census-wide identifier list.
#
# Nothing in this file is a measurement: it is file names and one-way digests.
# --- deposit: every one of these must be present in a complete data root ---
